ātharvaṇo 'yaṃ kathito vidhis te saṃkṣepato bhārgavāṃśamukhya /
ataḥ paraṃ kiṃ kathayāmi tubhyaṃ tan me vadasvāyatalohitākṣa //
// iti śriviṣṇudharmottare dvitīyakhaṇḍe mārkaṇḍeyavajrasaṃvāde rāmaṃ prati puṣkaropākhyāne 'tharvavidhikathanaṃ nāma saptaviṃśatyuttaraśatatamo 'dhyāyaḥ //

meaning:
आथर्वणो ऽयं कथितो विधिस् ते संक्षेपतो भार्गवांशमुख्य /
अतः परं किं कथयामि तुभ्यं तन् मे वदस्वायतलोहिताक्ष //
// इति श्रिविष्णुधर्मोत्तरे द्वितीयखण्डे मार्कण्डेयवज्रसंवादे रामं प्रति पुष्करोपाख्याने ऽथर्वविधिकथनं नाम सप्तविंशत्युत्तरशततमो ऽध्यायः //

text_mt:
This procedure according to the Atharvaveda has been told to you in brief, O foremost of the lineage of the Bhārgavas.
What further shall I tell you beyond this? Tell me that, O long- and red-eyed one!

// Thus ends the one hundred and twenty-seventh chapter, entitled 'Explanation of the Atharvan Rules', in the narration of Puṣkara to Rāma, in the dialogue between Mārkaṇḍeya and Vajra, in the second book of the auspicious Viṣṇudharmottara Purāṇa.

there is indeed nothing that cannot be given to devotees, even the kingdom of the gods, surely;
and for those seized by the noose of death, falling into hell,
that lord of gods, as soon as remembered, turns them away from that fear.

Whoever recites, reads, hears, remembers, wears,
or proclaims this Aparājitā, the supreme Vaiṣṇava perfected Great Knowledge, will have no fear from fire, wind, thunderbolts, hail, or lightning;
no fear from the ocean;
no fear from seizing spirits;
no fear from thieves;
nor will there be any fear from wild beasts;

“Arise, do not fear, O knower of righteousness! Choose a boon as you desire!”
[Nārada said:] “O Lord, let there be constant, exclusive devotion to you;
I desire no other boon; this is the highest for mortals.”
